TI  - Who is scientific code for? Maintaining human-readable landmarks in agent-written code
AU  - Elle O'Brien
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-07-28
AB  - Scientific research involving code has long rested on the assumption that at least one person understands why the code exists. As scientists adopt coding agents, this assumption is breaking down. Drawing on an ongoing contextual inquiry of scientific programmers working with agentic tools (four cases to date), a survey of over 800 scientific programmers, and my own analysis workflows, this position piece describes how scientists are inventing personal conventions, "landmarking strategies", for marking which artifacts in a codebase are meant for human understanding and which are context for agents. They repurpose shared infrastructure (version control especially) in idiosyncratic ways, and I argue that this quiet de-standardization could complicate collaboration in teams with heterogeneous software practices. Alternatively, teams that explicitly delineate what is human-readable versus agent context will be better able to develop, document, and maintain scientific code.
